<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imam Ali Shrine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first structure <a href="/years/786">786</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Mosque of Ali, Haram of Imam Ali</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> Ali Shrine is a Shia mosque and mausoleum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, which Shia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims believe holds the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a>, cousin and son-in-law of Muhammad. Shias regard Ali as the first Imam and Muhammad's rightful successor; Sunnis count him as the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. The Abbasid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> built the first structure over the tomb in 786 CE, and later rulers rebuilt and enlarged it, among them the Buyid emir '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, who gilded the dome in 1742. The present building dates from the Safavid period, with a dome 42 metres high between twin minarets. Millions of pilgrims visit each year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imam-ali-shrine.jpg" alt="Imam Ali Shrine" width="330" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Navy photo by Photographer's Mate 1st Class Arlo K. Abrahamson. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meshed_ali_usnavy_(PD).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
